      "problem": "Given that $P(x) = 2 \\cdot 5^{3x},$ find $x$ such that $P(x)=250.$",
      "worked_solution": "First we substitute $P(x)=250$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 5^{3x}= 250\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot {5}^{3x}}{2} & =\\frac{250}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}}{\\cancel{2}} & =125 \\\\ {5}^{3x} & =125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ using the fact that $125$ is a power of $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3x} & ={5}^{3} \\\\ 3x & =3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
